      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright 2010 Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "Published studies on birdshot chorioretinopathy (BCR) did not provide definitive information on possibly associated extraocular manifestations."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Single-center cross-sectional analysis of extraocular manifestations in a cohort of patients with BCR."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Since 2002, 118 patients (45 men, 73 women) were enrolled. Their mean age was 51.5 years at diagnosis. The most common features of their medical histories were: hypertension (32 patients), drug allergy (19), sinusitis (17), thyroid disease (12), otitis media (11), asthma (11); diabetes (10); cancer (8); psoriasis (5); monoclonal gammopathies (3). At the time of disease onset, arthralgias were noted in 23, ENT manifestations in 26, Raynaud's phenomenon in 6, headaches in 10, psoriasis in 3 others. Between diagnosis and cross-sectional evaluation visits, only the frequency of hypertension has increased significantly (11 additional patients)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "No predominant extraocular manifestation of BCR was identified in our patients. Their ongoing follow-up may yet discern whether BCR is definitively eye-restricted."
          }
        ]
      },
